WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.349 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  when i was five years old and my elder  sister was just starting  school my dad quit his steady job  he came home and told my mum i'm going  to start my own business  my mum burst into tears  they had two kids and a mortgage how  were they to make ends meet  my dad didn't come from immense  privilege he never earned a bachelor's  degree  but he was hungry for success and wanted  better for his children  so he scrapped together all of his  savings and invested everything into his  new advertising business  after five years of hard work the  business was struggling  and his co-founders quit undeterred  my dad remortgaged our house to raise  funds and pressed on without them  over the next 20 years he painstakingly  bootstrapped his business  taking measured risks and working  relentlessly to ensure the success of  his organization  he worked tirelessly to provide a better  life for his family  and today his business employs over 50  people  although my accent portrays the fact  that my dad's success was in australia  rather than america my dad lived his own  version  of the american dream  but my dad was one of the lucky ones we  know that today  for most people the american dream is a  myth  64 of the wealth of the united states is  held by the top 10  of families a child's chance of earning  more than his or her parents  has plummeted from 90 to 50  and the key determinant of your  financial outcome in life  is whether you were born into a wealthy  family  america has a long history of systemic  racism and unequal access to opportunity  many remedies have been proposed and  many remedies are needed  but i'm a tax nerd and from my  perspective  one important remedy is to reshape our  tax code  so that we focus on taxing wealth rather  than income  yes my friends this is a talk about  taxes  my father accumulated his wealth through  hard work  and he paid his fair share of income  taxes  but our tax code can't end there  his net his wealth now self perpetuates  more wealth  and i as his son and one of the  undeserving  beneficiaries so how does a tax  nerd like myself propose to change this  dynamic  i want to suggest two possible wealth  taxes  first an inheritance tax  an inheritance tax also known as an  estate tax  is a tax on the assets transferred from  deceased persons  to their heirs inheritance taxes  have a long history augustus the first  roman emperor  imposed the tax on 1 20th of inheritance  in 6 a.d  in the u.s the first inheritance tax was  adopted in 1797  but recently inheritance taxes have been  on the decline  in 1976 the highest inheritance tax  in america was 77 percent  today it is just 40 percent  simultaneously the exemption threshold  the amount that can be passed down  tax-free has continuously risen  with the tax reform in 2017 the tax-free  threshold  for estate taxes is 22.8 million dollars  for married couples  let me say that again today if you are a  married couple with less than 22.8  million dollars in assets  you don't pay a dollar of estate tax  some see this situation as justified  there is a righteous belief  among some that american freedom demands  that people should be able to hand their  private property to their children  and that any estate tax represents  dreaded double taxation  they say my parents have already paid  tax on these assets  why should they have to pay them again  but these arguments overlook the fact  that a significant portion of family  wealth is earned from  unrealised capital gains that is the  increase in the value of  assets like stocks and bonds  these unrealized capital gains have  never been taxed  and more importantly the people who  inherit the estate  have done nothing to earn this  inheritance  i ask again why am i entitled to the  fruits of my dad's hard work  letting fortunes pass across generations  risks creating  a hereditary elite this is antithetical  to america's spirit of rewarding  enterprise  above bloodline the constitution  prohibits the u.s government from  granting  titles of nobility so why should  americans allow nobility  in all but title  for those like myself who treasure the  dynamism and innovation of the american  economy  and inheritance tax is one of the most  efficient ways for the government to  raise revenue  unlike income taxes they do not reduce  the incentive to work unlike capital  gains taxes  it doesn't dissuade saving or investment  and  unlike sales taxes it's progressive  as we search for efficient ways to help  close the wealth gap  inheritance taxes are part of the answer  in 2019 american raised just 17 billion  dollars from its inheritance tax  if instead we were willing to tax  estates above a million dollars  with a progressive rate structure  starting at 30 percent  and going up to 75 percent for estates  above 20 million dollars  we would be able to raise approximately  130 billion dollars  annually  but inheritance tax by itself is not  enough  and economists have another tool to help  close the wealth gap  we also need to implement a national  land value tax  a land value tax is an annual percentage  tax  say three percent on the value of only  the land  it is different from a property tax  because it excludes the value of the  house or building sitting on the land  economists love land taxes  in 1978 milton friedman declared it the  least  bad tax and even earlier henry george's  book on land taxes  progress and poverty sold more copies in  america  in the 1890s than any other book  except the bible the reason for this  popularity  at least among economists is that  typically taxing a good  lowers supply and raises price it's for  this reason  that we tack cigarettes to deter smoking  or tax gasoline to deter frivolous  driving  but land is different in that its supply  is fixed  and as long as land owners are competing  with each other for renters  whose numbers and willingness to pay are  unaffected  the tax should not be passed on in  higher rents  the other reason taxing land values is  so adored by nerdy economist  is its fairness no person has a moral  claim on the land itself  and when the government builds roads  installs lights  and expands transport lengths the value  of land  increases without any effort from the  landowner  no landowner deserves these windfall  gains  the value of land in america is  estimated at 23  trillion dollars a national land value  tax of 3 percent  would raise almost 700 billion dollars  for the government  every single year this could be used to  help close the wealth gap  improving education for our children and  health care  for us all i'm not delusional  these tax reforms alone won't end  inequality  inequality is a deeply rooted issue in  our society  that will take a coalition of  politicians advocates  and business leaders years of hard work  to change  but i'm hopeful that tax reforms can be  a part of the solution  my mother and father should enjoy the  wealth that they have created  over a lifetime of hard work but while  they are alive  a land value tax should ensure that  their wealth is not enriched by  windfall gains of property prices and  when they pass away  i hope i live in a society where any  wealth they leave behind  is donated or taxed  [Music]

